A||||12||CUTTER||normal price for brakepads?||||1||
Z||000000||CUTTER||05-18-2000||12:37 AM||||I need new brakepads all the way around and dont know how much it cost. How much should I be exspecting?<BR>Thanks, shane||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||05-18-2000||01:49 AM||||Just the pads?  El Cheapo brand X pads only run about $10/set, but premium pads go for as much as $40/set.  <P>If you mean a brake job (all 4 wheels), then figure $10 or so, to turn each rotor ($40) + the pads($20-$80) + a little brake fluid ($2...if needed) + repacking bearings $??....if it's an '84-'87).....it could be as much as $125 or as little as $60, depending on the quality of the parts.  This asumes you do the work yourself.<P>If you bring it somewhere figure about $200 per axle, or $400+ for the whole car.  I was quoted $260 per axle for my '88 GT at a Goodyear store.  I wound up buying rebuilt calipers with premium pads and brand new rotors for half that much.  I did the job myself and it was a piece of cake.  Of course my GT came from California so there was no rust to contend with.....you may have problems getting your calipers off.<P>I hope this helps.||||reg||5
Z||000002||Standard||05-18-2000||03:57 AM||||I think the average cost is around $35 bucks for a set front or rear.  That's what I usually pay, for raybestos i think it is.||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||05-18-2000||09:15 AM||||<BR>I ended up paying about 30.00 for typical Raybestos pads on the rear axles.  I was looking for a better quality pad called super stops, but they couldn't find them.  They would have cost closer to 50.00 bucks for the rear axles.  Next time I'll look harder for a better set.  I was unfortunatly in a hurry to get it back on the road.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||05-18-2000||09:44 AM||||OK, here's a question borne of ignorance.  Unless your rotors are scored or warped, why turn them?  Won't that just shorten their usable life?||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||05-18-2000||02:07 PM||||I just finished a front brake job last month...Heres what I recall as far as pricing goes.<P>Pads: $15 per side<BR>Caliper: $35 each.<BR>Rotors: $40<BR>Hoses: $27 each<BR>Fluid: $2<BR>Grease: $3<BR>Bearings: $10 each<P>I did the Back Brakes last fall and I think these prices are pretty close<P>Calipers: $55 each<BR>Pads: $15 per side<BR>Rotors: $60 each<BR>Hoses: $20 each<P>All prices are from Discount Auto except for the Hoses which came from NAPA.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ka4nkf||05-18-2000||06:17 PM||||Don't turn the rotors unless they are warped. you can tell if they are warped by driving and apply pressure on brake pedal, if the steering wheel wobles, then you need to turn them.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Frank R||05-19-2000||02:42 AM||||Turning of disc brake rotors  is required because of a varety of reasons.  The most prominent reason to have the rotors turned is to remove the glazing on the surface of the rotors.  The temps of rotors are subjected to  some very high temps,which can cause the cast iron to have a glaze and not have a good co-efficient for friction.  Rotors that are slightly out of true actually are not really bad as this action helps the pads to retract from dragging on the rotors.  Disc brakes are very efficient and the braking action is always a question of which material will be sacrificed, either the rotors or the pad material.  Some friction material is always softer or harder than the other.  If both pads and rotors were very hard, not much braking would take place.  Frank R..||||reg||6
Z||000008||Sage||05-19-2000||10:01 AM||||Just bought front and rear pads(bonded Albany (made in Canada)lifetime warranty) can of brake cleaner and spray on can of anti squeal-chatter coat(which there is enough left of to do several more brake jobs with) total cost $37.50 @ Autozone. Turn rotors; $7.50ea. Cost to do all four wheels; $67.50 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Make sure your calipers are free to slide on the sleeves! (Use silicon grease to lube)||||reg||1
Z||000009||mshill||05-19-2000||10:08 AM||||Just bought rotors from wrenchhead.com and they weremuch less expensive than Autozon or Kragens: $24.37 for the front and $12.33 for the rears.  That's less than $75 for all four wheels.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||05-19-2000||11:07 AM||||There's just something about hunting around for the cheapest brakes. Kinda like buying the cheapest bullet proof vest.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||05-19-2000||01:58 PM||||Nicely said, Phil.<P>I'm thinking if you need all 4 corners done, why not spring for a complete Beretta upgrade.  It can be done for less than $250.  (Unless you have a stick or live in one of the backwards, retarded states that requires an inspection--you'll loose your Ebrake.)||||reg||1
Z||000012||87GTJohn||05-21-2000||12:16 PM||||Just did the rear on my 88 GT, $13 for the pads, lifetime warranty.  Both front and rear use same pads, at least on 88's.  Car would not pass inspection prior to changing pads and lubing sliders (outside pads were binding).  After the change and lubing everything worked great.  Pads were from AutoZone.  Hope this helps.<BR>87GTJohn ||||reg||1
